\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Schematic of the oppositeness computation, shown in a simplified two-dimensional PCA space. The query point $\mathbf  {z}_i$ (blue circle) is reflected by negating its first principal component, producing the ``opposite'' $\tilde  {\mathbf  {z}}_i$ (red diamond). The oppositeness score is the cosine distance from $\tilde  {\mathbf  {z}}_i$ to the nearest real corpus point (circled). When the original point lies in a directionally asymmetric region---dense on one side, sparse on the other---its opposite lands far from any real data, yielding a high oppositeness score. In the actual computation, 10 PCA components are used and the top 3 are negated.}}{42}{figure.caption.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:oppositeness}{{4.1}{42}{Schematic of the oppositeness computation, shown in a simplified two-dimensional PCA space. The query point $\mathbf {z}_i$ (blue circle) is reflected by negating its first principal component, producing the ``opposite'' $\tilde {\mathbf {z}}_i$ (red diamond). The oppositeness score is the cosine distance from $\tilde {\mathbf {z}}_i$ to the nearest real corpus point (circled). When the original point lies in a directionally asymmetric region---dense on one side, sparse on the other---its opposite lands far from any real data, yielding a high oppositeness score. In the actual computation, 10 PCA components are used and the top 3 are negated}{figure.caption.9}{}}
